  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 152 AM CDT Sun Oct 4 2026\n\nA potent mid-level wave crossing the northern Great Lakes early\nthis morning continues to encounter dry mid-level air across \nthe CWA. However, the strong forcing interacting with little \navailable moisture below modestly steep lapse rates has yielded \nan axis of isolated showers strong enough to result in precip \nreaching the surface north of I-88/290 corridor. This activity \nwill quickly move east of the area over the next couple hours.\n\nA cold front associated with the mid-level wave is charging \neastward across central Iowa at this time and is expected to \ncross the CWA from northwest to southeast with no precip and \nfew, if any, clouds mid-morning. CAA behind the front will \noffset some warming this afternoon, but upstream dew points in \nthe upper 30s to mid 40s and NW gusts around 20 mph may result \nin modest mixing and allow temps to tag the low to mid 70s.\n\nHigh pressure over Minnesota and Iowa tonight will cross the \narea Monday afternoon before reaching the eastern Great Lakes \nMonday night. Low temps in the low to mid 40s will be common \ntonight and Monday night outside of the core of the Chicago \nmetro, with typically colder spots potentially falling into the \nupper 30s.\n\nBroad longwave troughing across the eastern third of Canada \nwill maintain generally WNW flow aloft across the area through \nthe remainder of the upcoming week. After another dry cold \nfrontal passage on Wednesday, quiet conditions will persist into\nearly next weekend. Precip Tuesday through Saturday will be \nconfined to a low chance of light rain with a moisture-starved \nwave lifting northeastward across the forecast area late \nThursday night into Friday. Temps will turn seasonably warm late\nin the week into next weekend ahead of deep troughing \ndeveloping across the western CONUS early next week.\n\nKluber",
